    "justification": "The result identifies the observable separation scale at which honest abstention disappears.",
    "gap": "Lewis studies weak heteroskedastic identification for continuous structural parameters; the present boundary concerns the entire discrete compulsory-and-possible target classification over a nonclosed causal fiber.",
    "consumer": "Analysts obtain a diagnostic for when an exact target classification, rather than an outer family, is statistically defensible.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(\\theta\\in\\Theta_K\\) and write\n\\[\n Z=\\lVert\\widehat\\Sigma-\\theta\\rVert_w.\n\\]\nOn the event \\(Z\\le x\\), every \\(\\zeta\\in W_n\\) satisfies, by the triangle inequality,\n\\[\n \\lVert\\zeta-\\theta\\rVert_w\n \\le\\lVert\\zeta-\\widehat\\Sigma\\rVert_w\n   +\\lVert\\widehat\\Sigma-\\theta\\rVert_w\n \\le c_\\alpha+x. \\tag{26}\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\theta\\in\\Theta_K\\), the infimum in the approximate-selector inequality is at most \\(Z\\).  The strict tolerance-one guarantee therefore gives\n\\[\n \\lVert\\widetilde\\theta_n-\\widehat\\Sigma\\rVert_w<Z+1,\n \\qquad\n \\lVert\\widetilde\\theta_n-\\theta\\rVert_w<2Z+1\\le2x+1. \\tag{27}\n\\]\nSince \\(R_n=W_n\\cup\\{\\widetilde\\theta_n\\}\\), (26)--(27) prove\n\\[\n \\sup_{\\zeta\\in R_n}\\lVert\\zeta-\\theta\\rVert_w\n \\le\\max\\{x+c_\\alpha,2x+1\\}. \\tag{28}\n\\]\n\nWe compare the weighted norm with the frozen information norm.  For a symmetric tuple \\(H=(H_e)_e\\), set\n\\(K_e=\\Sigma_e^{-1/2}H_e\\Sigma_e^{-1/2}\\).  Then\n\\[\n \\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma_e^{-1}H_e\\Sigma_e^{-1}H_e)=\\lVert K_e\\rVert_F^2.\n\\]\nThe covariance bounds \\(mI_d\\preceq\\Sigma_e\\preceq MI_d\\), together with\n\\(H_e=\\Sigma_e^{1/2}K_e\\Sigma_e^{1/2}\\), imply\n\\[\n \\frac1M\\lVert H_e\\rVert_F\\le\\lVert K_e\\rVert_F\n \\le\\frac1m\\lVert H_e\\rVert_F.\n\\]\nAfter multiplication by \\(n_e\\), summation, and taking square roots,\n\\[\n \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2M}\\lVert H\\rVert_w\n \\le\\lVert H\\rVert_{I,n,\\theta}\n \\le\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2m}\\lVert H\\rVert_w. \\tag{29}\n\\]\n\nSuppose\n\\[\n \\max\\{x+c_\\alpha,2x+1\\}<\\sqrt2m\\,\\delta_n(\\theta).\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\zeta\\in R_n\\cap\\Theta_K\\), equations (28)--(29) give\n\\[\n \\lVert\\zeta-\\theta\\rVert_{I,n,\\theta}\n \\le\\frac{\\lVert\\zeta-\\theta\\rVert_w}{\\sqrt2m}\n <\\delta_n(\\theta). \\tag{30}\n\\]\nBy \\(\\text{def:classification-information-distance}\\), no such \\(\\zeta\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal A_h(\\theta)\\).  Hence every point of \\(R_n\\cap\\Theta_K\\) has classification \\(h(\\theta)\\).  This intersection is nonempty by \\(\\text{thm:honest-family-confidence}\\), so \\(\\mathcal H_n=\\{h(\\theta)\\}\\), and the singleton rule reports correctly.\n\nThe second-moment calculation (21)--(23), now with threshold \\(x\\), yields\n\\[\n \\Pr_\\theta(Z>x)\\le\\frac{\\mathbb E_\\theta Z^2}{x^2}\n \\le\\frac{V_{\\max}}{x^2}. \\tag{31}\n\\]\nThis is the stated failure bound.  Taking \\(x=c_\\alpha\\), the right side of (31) is \\(\\alpha\\), while the radius in (28) is at most \\(2c_\\alpha+1\\).  Therefore \\(\\sqrt2m\\delta_n(\\theta)>2c_\\alpha+1\\) implies correct reporting with probability at least \\(1-\\alpha\\).\n\nFinally, if \\(\\delta_n(\\theta_n)\\to\\infty\\), choose deterministic \\(x_n\\to\\infty\\) with \\(x_n/\\delta_n(\\theta_n)\\to0\\), interpreting the ratio as zero when the denominator is infinite.  The strict separation condition then holds eventually, and (31) tends to zero.  For a subclass with\n\\[\n D_n=\\inf_{\\theta\\text{ in the subclass}}\\delta_n(\\theta)\\longrightarrow\\infty,\n\\]\none may take \\(x_n=\\sqrt{\\min\\{D_n,n\\}}\\).  This common choice tends to infinity and satisfies \\(x_n/D_n\\to0\\), so the same argument is uniform over the subclass.",
